<span class="section-num">12.</span>

<button class="hebrew-toggle" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('visible')">☰ Hebrew § 12</button>
<div class="hebrew-block">וְזֶה שֶׁמּוּבָא בַּמִּדְרָשׁ, שֶׁמֹּשֶׁה רַבֵּנוּ עָלָיו הַשָּׁלוֹם, רָצָה לִכְנֹס לְאֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל עַל-יְדֵי בְּחִינַת הַתָּרַת נְדָרִים, כְּמוֹ שֶׁאָמְרוּ רַבּוֹתֵינוּ זַ״ל עַל פָּסוּק, אַתָּה הַחִלּוֹתָ, כִּי בְּחִינַת הַתָּרַת נְדָרִים הִוא בְּחִינַת הַדֶּרֶךְ שֶׁל אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל וְכַנַּ״ל. כַּמְבֹאָר בַּמַּאֲמָר הַנַּ״ל. שֶׁכָּל הַנַּ״ל הוּא בְּחִינַת הַדֶּרֶךְ לְאֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל, עַיֵּן שָׁם הֵיטֵב:</div>

<p class="first">And this is what is brought in the Midrash — that Moshe Rabbeinu, peace be upon him, wanted to enter Eretz Yisra'ail through the aspect of <em>hataras nedarim</em> <span class="added">[annulment of vows]</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory said on the verse, <span class="verse-inline">"You have begun…"</span> <span class="verse-source">(Deuteronomy 3:24)</span> <span class="added">[interpreting "<em>hachiloisa</em>" as related to "<em>hatarah</em>" — annulment, based on "<em>lo yachail devaro</em>" (Numbers 30:3)]</span>. For the aspect of <em>hataras nedarim</em> is the aspect of the <strong>path to Eretz Yisra'ail</strong>, as mentioned above. As is explained in the aforementioned discourse: that all of the above is the aspect of the path to Eretz Yisra'ail — see there well.</p>

<hr>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════ TRANSLATOR SUMMARY ══════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="translator-summary">
<h3>Translator's Summary</h3>

<p>This halacha, based on Likutay Moharan II:78 ("<em>Va'eschanan</em> — The Conduct of Simplicity"), presents a comprehensive kabbalistic framework explaining why a person has the power to create new Torah-law through vows and oaths, how these relate to the concealed Torah, and the spiritual mechanics connecting nedarim, nidah, mikveh, teshuvah, and the path to Eretz Yisra'ail.</p>

<p><strong>Core Teaching:</strong> All things in the world receive life-force from the Torah. The Torah exists in two modes: (1) the <em>concealed Torah</em> — the divine light hidden within creation since before Matan Torah, sustained by <em>chesed chinam</em> (gratuitous kindness), corresponding to the aspect of <em>Binah</em> / the hidden world; and (2) the <em>revealed Torah</em> — given at Sinai, channeled through the seven <em>midos</em> / seven days of construction, corresponding to <em>shevuah</em> (oath, from <em>sheva</em> = seven).</p>

<p><strong>Neder vs. Shevuah:</strong> A <em>neder</em> accesses the concealed Torah (above the seven <em>midos</em>), which is why it takes effect on <em>mitzvah</em>-matters, requires no Divine Name, and the object itself is forbidden ("the object is forbidden upon him"). A <em>shevuah</em> accesses the revealed Torah (within the seven <em>midos</em>), which is why it does not take effect on <em>mitzvah</em>-matters, involves a Divine Name, and the person forbids himself ("he forbids himself upon the object"). Both reveal that Torah permeates all things.</p>

<p><strong>The Tzadik's Role:</strong> This power to make Torah from all worldly things depends on the great ones of the generation. The great <em>tzadik</em>, in his mode of "simplicity" (when he ceases from overt Torah study), sustains himself and the world from the concealed Torah. From him, this power cascades to all Israel — hence "Moshe spoke to the <em>heads</em> of the tribes" specifically, and hence the <em>chacham</em> can uproot a <em>neder</em> from its root.</p>

<p><strong>Nidah – Mikveh – Teshuvah – Kol Nidray:</strong> The sin of Adam HaRishon introduced the Serpent's contamination (<em>dam nidos</em>), forcing the hidden light to be stored away so the <em>klipos</em> would not draw excessive sustenance. Purification requires: (1) seven clean days (corresponding to the seven <em>midos</em> where impurity's primary hold lies), then (2) immersion in the mikveh (= Binah = hidden light = chesed chinam = waters that preceded creation = 40 se'ah = 40 days of Torah). This same dynamic underlies <em>teshuvah</em> and Yom HaKipurim / <em>Kol Nidray</em> — all accessing the concealed Torah where no blemish reaches and from which one can return even from the lowest depths. All of this is the "path to Eretz Yisra'ail."</p>

